+**Status of this section (added 2026-11-11, `severity_is_ordinal` round; founder
+ruling of 2026-11-11 that the in-model severity quantity "becomes illustrative
+... rather than being definitive").** Three clarifications; no formula above is
+changed.
+
+1. **What §7.7 measures.** The product `W(class) · confidence · V(verification)`
+   contains one two-valued consequence factor (`W`) and two belief factors. By
+   caution 3 it bounds consequence magnitude nowhere above `W(SOFT)`, and by the
+   2026-11-06 archive measurement its computed value reduces to
+   `V(verification)` alone. It is a **credence-style quantity carrying a
+   consequence name**, ILLUSTRATIVE of how a credence factor enters severity —
+   not a definitive cross-domain severity measure, and no single numbering can
+   be one: severity proper is an **ordinal** quantity whose scale the domain
+   supplies (Ordinal Severity amendment,
+   `bench/directives/universal/cdsfl_core_formal.md`, "Objective and
+   Diminishing Returns"). The model fixes the ORDER of consequences; a domain's
+   own validated scale (FMEA severity, CTCAE grade, CVSS, INES, or the field's
+   equivalent — a domain may carry SEVERAL such scales) supplies a particular
+   numbering. A frozen critical cut must then be carried as a LEVEL of the
+   scale in use, not as 0.7 linearly rescaled: with `x → lo + 0.7·(hi − lo)`,
+   0.7 lands on a level boundary on CVSS (7.0 = High) but between levels on
+   FMEA (7.3 on 1–10), CTCAE (3.8 on 1–5) and INES (4.9 on 0–7), so each
+   scale's critical level is a per-scale freeze, not an arithmetic consequence
+   of 0.7.
+
+2. **Symbol collision, resolved by renaming HERE.** This section's verification
+   factor `V(verification)` collides with `V(f) = materiality(f)` in
+   `cdsfl_core_formal.md` ("Objective and Diminishing Returns"), which denotes
+   a consequence — the opposite meaning. Where both are in scope, write this
+   section's factor **`V_ver`**. The tables above keep `V` for continuity with
+   archived discussion; `V_ver` is the disambiguated name.
+
+3. **Admissible statistics of severity.** Because severity proper is ordinal,
+   any statistic of severity used in gating must commute with every strictly
+   increasing relabelling: max, min, counts at-or-above a LEVEL, and middle
+   order statistics qualify; sums and means of severity magnitudes do not, and
+   the interpolated even-n median does not either (exact counterexample:
+   sample {1/4, 3/4} under φ(t) = t² gives median(φ(x)) = 5/16 ≠
+   φ(median(x)) = 1/4; SymPy exact arithmetic, corroborated with Wolfram
